{
  "concept": "For a projectile launched and landing at the same height, range depends on initial speed squared and on sin(2θ), so range quadruples when speed doubles, is maximized at 45°, and is identical for complementary launch angles even though their peak heights differ.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Panel (a) label and panel (b) label, centered beneath each panel",
    "Small axis key in upper right: short vertical arrow labeled y, short horizontal arrow labeled x",
    "Panel (a): horizontal ground line at y = 0 serving as both launch level and landing level",
    "Panel (a): common launch point at the left end of the ground line",
    "Panel (a): three launch velocity arrows from the launch point, all at 45°, of lengths proportional to speed — orange labeled '30 m/s', purple labeled '40 m/s', teal labeled '50 m/s'",
    "Panel (a): angle arc at the launch point labeled '45°'",
    "Panel (a): three parabolic trajectories in matching colors (orange shortest, purple medium, teal longest), each starting and ending on the ground line",
    "Panel (a): dashed vertical guide lines dropped from the apex of each parabola to the ground line",
    "Panel (a): three stacked dimension bars with end ticks and double-headed arrows below the ground line, labeled 'R = 91.8 m', 'R = 163 m', 'R = 255 m'",
    "Panel (b): horizontal ground line with a common launch point at the left",
    "Panel (b): label 'v0 = 50 m/s' near the launch arrows",
    "Panel (b): three launch arrows of equal length at 15° (orange), 45° (purple), 75° (teal), with nested angle arcs labeled '15°', '45°', '75°'",
    "Panel (b): three parabolas in matching colors — tall narrow teal (75°), broad purple (45°), low flat orange (15°)",
    "Panel (b): dashed vertical from the shared landing point of the 15° and 75° curves, and a dashed vertical from the apex of the 75° curve",
    "Panel (b): two dimension bars below the ground line labeled 'R = 128 m' (15° and 75°) and 'R = 255 m' (45°)",
    "Live readout text for the active panel: v0, θ, computed range R, apex height H, and time of flight"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "speedA",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Panel (a): initial speed v₀ (θ fixed at 45°)",
      "range": [10, 60, 1],
      "default": 30,
      "teaches": "With the launch angle held at 45°, range grows as the square of initial speed — doubling v₀ from 25 to 50 m/s makes the trajectory four times longer, not twice."
    },
    {
      "id": "angleB",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Panel (b): launch angle θ (v₀ fixed at 50 m/s)",
      "range": [5, 85, 1],
      "default": 45,
      "teaches": "At fixed speed, range follows sin(2θ): it rises to a single maximum at 45° and falls off symmetrically, so shallow and steep launches both fall short."
    },
    {
      "id": "showComplement",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Show complementary angle (90° − θ)",
      "default": false,
      "teaches": "Complementary angles land at exactly the same spot but take different paths — the steep one flies higher and longer in time, the shallow one flies faster downrange."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Reference case: 30 m/s at 45°",
      "narration": "Panel (a) starts with the orange textbook trajectory: 30 m/s launched at 45° travels 91.8 m. The dashed line marks the apex, and the bar below measures the range.",
      "state": { "speedA": 30, "angleB": 45, "showComplement": false }
    },
    {
      "title": "Raise the speed to 40 m/s",
      "narration": "Drag the speed slider up. Speed rose by a factor of 4/3, but range jumped from 91.8 m to 163 m — a factor of (4/3)², not 4/3.",
      "state": { "speedA": 40, "angleB": 45, "showComplement": false }
    },
    {
      "title": "Speed 50 m/s — range scales as v₀²",
      "narration": "At 50 m/s the projectile reaches 255 m. Comparing to 30 m/s: the speed ratio is 5/3 while the range ratio is 25/9. Range is proportional to v₀².",
      "state": { "speedA": 50, "angleB": 45, "showComplement": false }
    },
    {
      "title": "Now fix the speed and swing the angle",
      "narration": "In panel (b) the speed is locked at 50 m/s. At 45° the range is the same 255 m as before — this is the maximum any 50 m/s launch can achieve.",
      "state": { "speedA": 50, "angleB": 45, "showComplement": false }
    },
    {
      "title": "A shallow 15° launch",
      "narration": "Drop the angle to 15°. The path is flat and fast but lands at only 128 m — the projectile simply is not in the air long enough.",
      "state": { "speedA": 50, "angleB": 15, "showComplement": false }
    },
    {
      "title": "Complementary angles share a range",
      "narration": "Turn on the complement to draw 75° alongside 15°. The tall arc and the flat arc land on the very same dashed line at 128 m, even though their peak heights and flight times are wildly different.",
      "state": { "speedA": 50, "angleB": 15, "showComplement": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "Why 45° wins",
      "narration": "Sweep back toward 45° with the complement still shown: the two curves merge, because 45° is its own complement. That coincidence is exactly where sin(2θ) peaks and range is greatest.",
      "state": { "speedA": 50, "angleB": 45, "showComplement": true }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Range = v₀²sin(2θ)/g packs two separate lessons: speed enters squared (so it dominates), while angle enters through sin(2θ), which peaks at 45° and returns the same value for θ and 90° − θ.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Two stacked panels sharing a light gray horizontal ground line, launch point at the left edge of each panel, generous empty space to the right so long trajectories fit. Color code is fixed throughout: orange = slowest/shallowest, purple = middle/45°, teal = fastest/steepest. Panel (a) top: three 45° launch arrows of proportional length with speed labels stacked to the upper left ('30 m/s', '40 m/s', '50 m/s'), a small angle arc labeled '45°', three nested parabolas all starting at the origin and returning to the ground line, thin gray dashed verticals from each apex, and three stacked double-headed dimension bars with end ticks below the ground line reading 'R = 91.8 m', 'R = 163 m', 'R = 255 m' in increasing length. A small y-up / x-right axis key sits at the top right of panel (a). Panel (b) below: three equal-length arrows fanned at 15°, 45°, 75° with nested angle arcs labeled inside, 'v₀ = 50 m/s' text beside the steepest arrow, a tall narrow teal parabola, a broad purple parabola, and a low flat orange parabola; the teal and orange curves land on a shared dashed vertical labeled by the 'R = 128 m' bar, while the purple curve's landing is marked by the longer 'R = 255 m' bar. Labels use italic R and subscripted v₀. The active (slider-driven) curve should be drawn heavier and fully opaque; the textbook reference curves stay as thin faded ghosts so the learner can compare against them. Show a compact live readout of v₀, θ, R, apex height H, and flight time near each panel."
}

